  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nPersistent ridging along with a passing warm front will bring \ntemperatures well above normal for the entire area through the rest \nof this week. Wednesday and Thursday will be the two warmest days \nbefore a cold front crosses the area, with highs in the upper 60s to \nlow 70s for most areas. Temperatures will cool each day for Friday \nthrough Sunday, first behind the cold front later Thursday, then \nwith another cold front and sfc low crossing the area on Saturday. \nWith the cooling temperatures, afternoon highs will approach, but are \nexpected to remain above normal for Sunday and Monday. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nThe cold front associated with an occluding sfc low will track \nacross the region on Thursday, bringing the best potential for \nwidespread showers in the past couple of weeks. Most of the showers \nwill move across the area during the early to mid morning hours, \namounting to a tenth to quarter of an inch of rainfall. The sfc low \nwill slowly track across the Great Lakes and to New England from \nlate Wednesday through Sunday, becoming vertically stacked with its \nmid and upper level lows. This will result in a prolonged period \nwhere showers will be possible at times within the cyclonic flow for \nmost of the area. Behind the passing cold front on Thursday dryer \nair will move into the region behind the passing cold front, \nproviding a break in the showers. There is some uncertainty among \nguidance for how much dry air moves into the region and how much the \nshowers diminish. As the mid & upper level lows/troughs approach and \nthen cross the area, the potential for showers will increase some to \nthe west with some increased potential for lake enhancement off of \nthe eastern Great Lakes as 850H temperatures cool. Shower potential \nwill increase late Friday into Saturday from west to east, from the \nmid and upper level lows and troughs crossing the area. Shower will \nlinger into Sunday morning. \n\nFor Friday into Saturday, a wave of low pressure will track along \nthe coast along the slow moving frontal boundary from Thursday, \nincreasing the potential for showers/rain for the far eastern areas. \nThere is still plenty of uncertainty at this point for how far west \nthe coastal wave will track, but most guidance keeps it just east of \nthe BGM forecast area. \n\nAnother trough dropping southeast out of Canada will bring another \nchance for showers late Sunday night into Monday, with lake \nenhancement adding to the shower potential.",
